PRETABLE 5 -- #531, kill table 5: the two repairs table 4 earned.
Written 2026-09-18 BEFORE table 5 runs; mtime is the order discriminator.
PRETABLE{,2,3,4}.out are NOT edited -- their mtimes are the order proofs for tables 1-4.

================================================================================
TABLE 4 SCORED 6 OF 8 AS PREDICTED.  BOTH MISSES WERE MINE, AND THEY ARE
DIFFERENT KINDS OF WRONG.
================================================================================
  V1 31/32 xbit rev              KILL as predicted
  V3 31/32 latch 1x              KILL as predicted
  V4 31/32 latch 28wr            KILL as predicted
  V5 31/32 latch 30wr            KILL as predicted
  V6 30/32 latch tick + xbit rev KILL, and by TWO rows rather than the one predicted
  V7 32/32 ESCAPE                as predicted -- the DISCLOSED counter debt, escaping
                                 correctly.  A written prediction of an escape landing is
                                 worth as much as a kill landing: it says the disclosure
                                 is honest rather than a hedge.

  *** V2 32/32 ESCAPED -- A ROW I BUILT WRONG. ***
  A clobber can only bite if the clobbered class's bit was ALREADY SET.  `xbit isr' runs
  TICK -> ISR, so when the tick assigns, the ISR bit has never been set, nothing is lost,
  and the row is green on the mutant.  I built one collision direction correctly and gave
  its mirror the opposite ORDER but not the opposite OPERAND-AND-COUNT.
  *** That is the #499 shape a third time this round, and it is the SAME defect the opus5
  seat diagnosed in the ORIGINAL `xbit', reproduced inside the repair for it. ***
  REPAIR: `xbit rev2' -- ISR -> TICK -> ISR, counting ISRMSG.  ADDED, not re-aimed:
  `xbit isr' still carries a real claim and re-aiming would trade one cell for another.

  *** V8 28/32 KILLED FOUR ROWS -- A MUTANT I BUILT WRONG, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT FAILURE
  FROM V2 AND MUST NOT BE FILED AS A SUCCESS. ***
  It was anchored on the FIRST two-tab `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)' in the file.  That is
  at :172, inside `case 0x10000' -- THE INTERRUPT MASK REGISTER, not 0x10020.  Every tick
  row arms bit 50 with a 64-bit `sd' (len 8), so `&& len == 4' REFUSED that store, bit 50
  was never set, no tick announced, and four tick rows reddened FOR A REASON UNRELATED TO
  THE CELL UNDER TEST.
  A mutant that dies for the wrong reason misleads exactly as much as one that will not
  die, and the carrier's three explanations apply: this is case 1, MIS-BUILT.
  REPAIR: anchor on guard #3 (:202, `case 0x10020'), verified by a dry-run diff.

  *** AND THE MIS-BUILD LEFT A REAL FINDING BEHIND, recorded rather than discarded: THE
  DETECTOR DOES CATCH A WIDTH NARROWING AT imask0, with a four-row signature. ***  That is
  coverage on an arm this round never enumerated -- the tick rows collectively pin that
  imask0 accepts a 64-bit write.  Not a floor cell (the fix does not touch imask0), but it
  is real and it was found by accident.

================================================================================
CHANNEL -- the three mutants, and the PREDICTIONS
================================================================================
  MUTANT          CELL                              ROW THAT MUST RED     PREDICT
  V2_assigntick   collision TICK->ISR (opus MD)     xbit rev2             KILL 32/33
  V8_len4         WIDTH at 0x10020 (opus MC)        -- none --            ESCAPE 33/33
  V1_assign20wr   REGRESSION: already killed        xbit rev              KILL 32/33

  V8 IS PREDICTED TO ESCAPE AND THAT IS NOT A BREACH: the body never tests `len', so by the
  pcc2widthkills bound it is a single-operand marginal -- clause (e) debt, not floor.  It is
  carried with opus5's cost note attached: `len' takes at most four values and the probe
  already has guest_store64/guest_load64, so the debt is CHEAP rather than unbounded.
  *** IF IT KILLS ANYWAY THAT IS A RESULT: a row would be observing something its own
  comment does not claim, and that row owes a re-reading. ***

  V1 IS RE-RUN DELIBERATELY.  #462 measured that adding rows perturbs other mutants' scores,
  and `xbit rev2' is a new row in the same class as `xbit rev'.  My claim that an added
  independent session cannot perturb V1 is REASONED, and V1 is its single test -- the same
  discipline table 2 applied to M1 and found true.

================================================================================
WHAT WOULD CLOSE THE CHAIN
================================================================================
Base 33/33; V2 and V1 killed by exactly their named rows; V8 escaping as disclosed.  Then
every cell in the stated enumeration is either KILLED or DISCLOSED WITH A RULING, and the
fable5 fork's first branch is satisfied.  An escape OUTSIDE the enumeration still triggers
the wholesale drop rather than a pass six -- that hatch remains unspent.
